R&C patch 7112: the fragment-program decompiler's NRM leaves a zero vector as it is (no NaN).

Idea from Re-TeRo17/ps3recomp 93dbfdd ("fp decompiler: NRM leaves a zero vector as it is", MIT); hand rewrite.

libs/video/rsx_fp_decompiler.c emitted NRM as float4(normalize(a.xyz), 1.0). HLSL normalize(0) is 0 * rsqrt(0) = NaN
on every lane, so a normal that blends to zero on some pixels poisons everything computed from it (in Drakengard 3 the
NaN reached the HDR scene and the bloom spread it into black blocks; the fork found it on a capture where exactly those
764 pixels changed). RPCS3 guards the same op: FragmentProgramDecompiler.cpp emits _builtin_normalize(a.xyz).xyzz with
#define _builtin_normalize(x) (length(x) > 0? normalize(x) : x) (read for behaviour, nothing copied)
[src:RPCS3, read 2026-11-19 via raw URL, master]. The fork commit is [src:tod-recomp-retero17].

Fix: float4(dot(a.xyz, a.xyz) > 0.0 ? normalize(a.xyz) : a.xyz, 1.0). dot > 0 is RPCS3's length > 0 without the
sqrt. w stays 1.0 as this decompiler had it; RPCS3 writes w = the normalized z (.xyzz). ASSUMPTION: neither w nor the
exact zero result is checked against hardware. A NaN source still gives NaN (the compare is false, the source passes
through), as before. The source is repeated four times; one too long for the 640-byte rhs buffer fails the decompile
(-1) rather than emit a truncated line, as 7067 does for LIT/RFL.

Test: port/rc/tests/run_fp_nrm_zero_test.sh (test_fp_nrm_zero.c): one hand-assembled NRM instruction; the HLSL text has
the guarded select with w = 1.0, no unguarded normalize, and the xy write mask and saturate still go through the common
tail. Text checks only (no host HLSL evaluator). Negative control (the series without this patch): FAIL, 2 failures
(guarded form missing, unguarded normalize present).

--- a/libs/video/rsx_fp_decompiler.c
+++ b/libs/video/rsx_fp_decompiler.c
@@ -1146,7 +1146,18 @@
                      a, a, b);
             break;
         case OP_DP2: snprintf(rhs, sizeof(rhs), "dot((%s).xy, (%s).xy)", a, b); break;
-        case OP_NRM: snprintf(rhs, sizeof(rhs), "float4(normalize((%s).xyz), 1.0)", a); break;
+        /* R&C patch 7112: NRM of a zero vector is the zero vector, not NaN. HLSL normalize(0) is 0 * rsqrt(0) = NaN on
+         * every lane, and it runs on through the lighting (Drakengard 3: black blocks after bloom, Re-TeRo17 93dbfdd).
+         * RPCS3 guards the same way (FragmentProgramDecompiler.cpp _builtin_normalize: length > 0 ? normalize : x, read
+         * for behaviour). dot > 0 is the same test without the sqrt. RPCS3 writes w = normalized z (.xyzz); w stays 1.0
+         * here as before this patch (ASSUMPTION: neither checked against hardware). The source is repeated four
+         * times; one too long for the rhs buffer fails the decompile (-1) rather than emit a truncated line. */
+        case OP_NRM: {
+            const int len = snprintf(rhs, sizeof(rhs),
+                "float4(dot((%s).xyz, (%s).xyz) > 0.0 ? normalize((%s).xyz) : (%s).xyz, 1.0)", a, a, a, a);
+            if (len < 0 || (size_t)len >= sizeof rhs) return -1;
+            break;
+        }
         case OP_LRP: snprintf(rhs, sizeof(rhs), "lerp((%s), (%s), (%s))", c, b, a); break;
         /* Lighting coefficients: (1, y, y > 0 ? 2^w : 0, 1) (RPCS3
          * FragmentProgramDecompiler, RSX_FP_OPCODE_LIF). */
